  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3027743251b98968805564e296d7570d80d35ff64b27cf831e631ed9c8efb40a.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous?",
  "worked_solution": "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-3,0)$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-3$ and left-continuous at $x=0.$ Hence, it is continuous on the closed interval $[-3,0].$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $[-3,0]$ and $(0,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[-3,0]$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $[0,1]$ and $[0,4]$ contain the endpoint $x=0,$ but the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\n- $[-1,1]$ and $[-2,2]$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=0,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.",
